    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d56">

                <pb facs="0161" n="59" xml:id="pg59"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/5743: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Minister in China
                        (Johnson)</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1933-01-13T17:00:00-05:00">January 13, 1933—5
                        p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>11. Your 45, January 13, 11 a.m. Alfred Sze has twice approached the Department
                    on this subject, and officers of the Department have replied tentatively that in
                    view of the clarity and definiteness with which this Government has on many
                    occasions set forth its views there seems no warrant for doubt with regard to
                    our attitude, and, because of various factors in the situation, it may
                    reasonably be doubted whether a new public statement on our own initiative at
                    this time would be appropriate or serve any useful purpose. The Department is
                    keeping in close touch with Sze.</p>
                <p>Please instruct Peck to make reply orally to Soong along the above lines.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Stimson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
